{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"2cceb5663ac4dc8030cd0d16a65e086b8b1541fcfea2fd44918d750c790fed99"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's distinguishing criterion—covariance of panel errors with checklist-irrelevant, outcome-relevant features—fails when a checklist omits an expert-unique cue but no observable proxy for that cue is recorded in the evaluation. The residual analogy presupposes the omitted variable is available to test against; if the omitted cue is latent or unmeasured, the covariance is untestable in a single evaluation, leaving no way to distinguish compliance-tracking from genuine coverage in exactly the cases where the expert's cue-set exceeds the checklist.","target":"46a563313cf630b9a927bf13308a690810230fb7a9204d45341719a864924e04"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's 'known generative model' requirement narrows the task class to atypical cases where the posterior is accessible, but the problem asks about tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes broadly—most such tasks (medical diagnosis, fault prediction) have verifiable outcomes yet unknown generative models. Under that scope, 'the checklist is necessarily a lossy compression of the generative model' is unsupported: without a known generative model the checklist's bias relative to it is undefined, so the claimed distinguishing evidence—systematic error on codec-divergent items—cannot be isolated in a single evaluation.","target":"7334c9c01f86141bef810a449cf2c6629099c96d93a99a7c89345293ab09bd6e"}]}